WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.460
SETH VARGO: In this episode
of the Secret Manager video

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:05.580
series, we'll talk about
global secret names

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:08.220
and regional
replication policies.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:11.340
Should you choose
user-managed or automatic?

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:12.630
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:15.035
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:25.470
In Secret Manager, secret names
are project global resources.

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:29.250
This is because secrets rarely
differ across cloud regions.

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:33.870
As a concrete example, your
Twilio or Twitter API key

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:37.890
doesn't change when you move
your application from US-EAST1

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:40.150
to EUROPE-WEST4.

00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:43.120
This is different from other
Google Cloud resources, which

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:46.720
have a tighter coupling to a
particular region or geography.

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:49.120
For example, in
Google Compute Engine.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.670
Virtual machine instances
are bound and referenced

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:54.430
by the zone in which
they're created.

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:57.730
Even though the secret's name
is a project global resource,

00:00:57.730 --> 00:01:01.060
the secret's payload is
still stored regionally.

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:03.130
This means that a
secret is globally

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:05.349
addressable by a
single name, but

00:01:05.349 --> 00:01:07.540
the underlying secret
material is still

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:09.910
stored in particular regions.

00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:12.640
Payload storage may be
an important concern

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:14.993
for regulated customers
or for customers

00:01:14.993 --> 00:01:16.660
that have strict
requirements over where

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:17.800
their data is stored--

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:20.530
like banking, fintech,
or health care.

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:22.870
Other customers may prefer
to have their data stored

00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:26.800
closer to where they plan to
access it, to minimize latency.

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:29.260
But how do you choose
the regions in which

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:31.450
the secret payloads are stored?

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:34.960
Google Secret Manager
features replication policies,

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:38.620
which give you the freedom
to choose how and where

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:40.720
your secret payload is stored.

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:43.520
To tell us a little bit more
about replication policies,

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:45.730
I think we need to
bring in an expert.

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:47.972
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:49.075 --> 00:01:49.660
Hey, Colin.

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:50.827
Thanks for joining us today.

00:01:50.827 --> 00:01:51.702
COLIN MAN: Hey, Seth.

00:01:51.702 --> 00:01:52.760
Thanks for having me.

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:55.120
SETH VARGO: So Colin is a
software engineer working

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:57.100
on Google Secret Manager.

00:01:57.100 --> 00:01:58.180
OK, Colin.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:01:59.950
Earlier, I was
telling our viewers

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:03.262
about data storage and
regionalization of Secrets.

00:02:03.262 --> 00:02:04.720
Could you tell us
a little bit more

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:07.160
about Secret Manager's
replication policies?

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:08.139
COLIN MAN: Sure, Seth.

00:02:08.139 --> 00:02:10.180
Replication policies
offer control

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:12.550
over where your secret
payloads are stored.

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:14.350
Some enterprises
want full control

00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:16.960
over the regions in which
their secrets are stored,

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:19.060
while others don't
have a preference.

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:21.970
Secret Manager addresses both
of these customer requirements

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:25.240
and preferences with the
replication policies.

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:27.820
If you don't care about
where your payload is stored,

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:30.730
you should choose an
automatic replication policy.

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:33.070
With automatic
replication, Google Cloud

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:36.280
chooses the best regions to
replicate your secret payload.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:39.670
This strategy also offers the
highest availability guarantees

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:42.440
for accessing and
creating secrets.

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:44.740
SETH VARGO: OK, Colin,
but which regions

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:48.400
does Secret Manager pick if I
choose automatic replication?

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:51.400
COLIN MAN: That's explicitly
unspecified behavior, Seth.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:55.893
You should interpret
automatic as I don't care.

00:02:55.893 --> 00:02:57.310
SETH VARGO: But
what if I do care?

00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:59.590
What if I care too
much about where

00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:01.122
my secret payload is stored?

00:03:01.122 --> 00:03:03.580
COLIN MAN: Then, you should
choose user-managed replication

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:04.540
policies.

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:08.020
With user-managed replication,
you choose one or more regions

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:10.240
to which to replicate
your secret payloads.

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:11.740
SETH VARGO: Yeah,
but then I'm going

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:14.110
to have to run some Cloud
Function that replicates

00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:15.970
my secret payload
and I have to deal

00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:18.430
with all the monitoring and
the alerting and the paging

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:18.670
and the 3:00 AM--

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:19.025
COLIN MAN: No, actually--

00:03:19.025 --> 00:03:19.810
SETH VARGO: --and
it's just like--

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:21.102
COLIN MAN: Actually, you won't.

00:03:21.102 --> 00:03:23.410
User-managed is more
like user selected.

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:26.170
You pick the regions and
Secret Manager automatically

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:28.550
handles the replication.

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:30.260
SETH VARGO: Oh,
that's super cool.

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:32.960
I'm just going to replicate
my secret payload into all the

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:34.540
available Google Cloud regions.

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:37.040
That's going to give me the
most availability and the lowest

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:37.700
latency.

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:38.570
Thanks, Colin.

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:41.042
COLIN MAN: Well, I'd
be careful with that.

00:03:41.042 --> 00:03:41.750
SETH VARGO: What?

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:42.688
Why is that?

00:03:42.688 --> 00:03:45.230
COLIN MAN: While replicating a
secret payload to every region

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:48.050
improves the reliability
of accessing a secret,

00:03:48.050 --> 00:03:52.260
it may decrease the reliability
of adding a secret version.

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:53.230
SETH VARGO: Ah.

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:55.170
OK, that makes sense.

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:56.750
Well, it-- wait.

00:03:56.750 --> 00:03:58.240
No, that-- that
doesn't make sense.

00:03:58.240 --> 00:03:59.187
Why is that?

00:03:59.187 --> 00:04:01.270
COLIN MAN: Well, in order
to add a secret version,

00:04:01.270 --> 00:04:04.210
all of the regions you
select must be operational.

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:07.030
If you choose a lot of
regions and any single one

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:09.400
of those regions is
down, you can no longer

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:12.160
add a new secret version.

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:13.000
SETH VARGO: I see.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:15.040
So depending on
my application, I

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:17.019
can choose to balance
my read availability

00:04:17.019 --> 00:04:18.860
and my write
availability accordingly.

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:19.959
COLIN MAN: Exactly.

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:22.480
SETH VARGO: So how many
regions should I choose, Colin?

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:23.900
COLIN MAN: Well,
as you mentioned,

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:26.020
it's heavily dependent
on the application.

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:29.540
As a general recommendation,
you should choose at least two

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:32.207
but no more than five regions.

00:04:32.207 --> 00:04:33.040
SETH VARGO: Awesome.

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:33.910
Thanks, Colin.

00:04:33.910 --> 00:04:35.530
COLIN MAN: No problem, Seth.

00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:36.400
SETH VARGO: OK.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:39.430
Let's look at how we set a
secret's replication policy

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:41.650
from the Google Cloud console.

00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:46.030
First, we choose the secret's
name, initial payload,

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:50.380
and then, we have the option
of selecting regionalization.

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:54.220
Notice that the default behavior
is automatic, since that's

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:56.320
what most customers will choose.

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:00.070
If I want to select specific
regions, I can check the box

00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:03.190
and pick the replicated
regions from the menu.

00:05:03.190 --> 00:05:05.710
I can also set
replication policies from

00:05:05.710 --> 00:05:08.590
the command-line--
using the gcloud tool--

00:05:08.590 --> 00:05:10.030
or from the API--

00:05:10.030 --> 00:05:16.150
using any of the Secret Manager
SDKs for .NET, Go, Java, PHP,

00:05:16.150 --> 00:05:19.830
Node, Python, and Ruby.

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:23.280
Google Secret Manager's
flexible replication policies

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:26.620
give you choice over where
your secrets are stored.

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:29.940
If you don't have specific
regionalization requirements,

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:32.610
choose the automatic
replication policy.

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:36.060
But if you do have specific
regionalization requirements,

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:38.400
pick the user-managed
replication policy

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:40.110
with specific regions.

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:43.050
For even more tips and tricks
with Google Secret Manager,

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:45.060
subscribe to Google
Cloud platform.

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:48.110
[MUSIC PLAYING]

